## Why This Question Is Asked
This question tests understanding of three-valued propagation on monotone constraints and the ability to design minimal backtracking strategies.

## Key Concepts
- Three-valued logic with ordering T > U > F
- Edge-wise monotone constraints on a directed graph
- Fixed-point propagation with backtracking for conflict resolution

## Code Example
```javascript
function propagate(nodes, edges, init) {
  const rank = { 'T': 2, 'U': 1, 'F': 0 };
  let changed = true;
  while (changed) {
    changed = false;
    for (const [from, to] of edges) {
      if (rank[nodes[to]] < rank[nodes[from]]) {
        nodes[to] = nodes[from];
        changed = true;
      }
    }
  }
  return nodes;
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to reason with Kleene 3-valued semantics in a concrete constraint system, plus practical propagation and backtracking.

## Key Concepts
- Kleene 3-valued logic: T>U>F; ¬U=U; a→b = (¬a) ∨ b; ∨/∧ as max/min.
- Constraint propagation: prune inconsistent values; arc-consistency.
- Backtracking: search space pruning when a constraint becomes F.

## Code Example
```javascript
// Kleene 3-valued semantics helpers (T=1, U=0.5, F=0)
const V = { T: 1, U: 0.5, F: 0 };
const notV = (v) => (v === V.T ? V.F : v === V.F ? V.T : V.U);
const orV = (a,b) => Math.max(a,b);
const andV = (a,b) => Math.min(a,b);
const implies = (a,b) => orV(notV(a), b);
```

